      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Why a paid audit, when the free one already exists</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/audit/" class="inline-link">free five-point audit</a> is exactly what it says it is, namely five points written up plainly with no sales call attached, and it is a real deliverable on its own terms. It also has a ceiling, in that it covers your homepage and one service page rather than the whole site, and it does not include a recorded walk-through.</p>
      <p>The Service Site Audit is the same posture taken deeper. The differences are below.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Five top pages</strong> instead of two. Homepage, top three service pages, top service-area page. Real Lighthouse runs on each, mobile + desktop.</li>
        <li><strong>Full schema audit.</strong> Every JSON-LD block on the site checked for syntax, completeness, GBP entity match, and missed opportunities.</li>
        <li><strong>Mobile-form audit.</strong> Every form on the site evaluated: field count, intake intelligence, urgency routing, accessibility, conversion friction.</li>
        <li><strong>Local-pack diagnostic.</strong> NAP consistency between site and Google Business Profile. Citation cross-check across major directories. Service-area page depth scored.</li>
        <li><strong>Conversion-flow review.</strong> Every CTA path traced. Every emergency / phone-click / form-fill flow tested on a real phone.</li>
        <li><strong>60-minute recorded walk-through.</strong> Not a written summary, a Loom video walking through every finding, on screen, with my voice explaining what to fix and what to leave alone.</li>
        <li><strong>"Fix these three" prioritization.</strong> The audit ends with a one-page priority list: the three things to fix this month, the three things to fix next quarter, and the three things to ignore.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who this is for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Service-business owners with an existing site spending real money on Google Ads or organic SEO and wondering why the leads are flat.</li>
        <li>Agencies and consultants who want a senior-developer second opinion on a client's site before recommending a rebuild.</li>
        <li>Buyers who would book a discovery call but want a deeper diagnostic in writing first.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How the work flows</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>You submit the form below.</strong> Web3Forms emails me your URL along with the business context, and Stripe sends the invoice. Payment is required for the work to start.</li>
        <li><strong>Lighthouse runs and on-page diagnostics.</strong> I run mobile and desktop Lighthouse against your top five pages, audit every JSON-LD schema block on the site, and cross-check your name, address, and phone consistency.</li>
        <li><strong>Conversion-flow review on a real phone.</strong> I walk every primary contact path on a physical mobile device and write down what I find about form friction, urgency routing, and the call-to-action ladder.</li>
        <li><strong>The report and the recorded walk-through.</strong> I draft the roughly twenty-five-page report in Google Docs and then record a sixty-minute Loom that walks through every finding on screen, in plain language.</li>
        <li><strong>Delivery to you.</strong> The finished PDF and the Loom link land in your inbox, and a thirty-minute Q&amp;A call is available on request with no extra cost attached.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Start to finish, the whole thing tends to take roughly two weeks, give or take.</p>

      <h2>Refund posture</h2>
      <p>If within sixty days of receiving the audit you decide to sign on for either the monthly plan or the lump-sum build, the $499 is credited back to you, either toward your first monthly invoice or off the lump-sum total. In effect, you only pay for the audit if you decide it stands on its own as a deliverable.</p>
      <p>If the audit is delivered and you genuinely do not feel it was worth what you paid for it, send me an email and I will refund the fee with no questions and no friction. The audit lives or dies on whether it teaches you something concrete about your site, and I would rather lose the fee than disagree with you about whether it did.</p>
